; The automatic eager policy considers every floating-point signature after
; every bit-vector one, and then selects it out of whatever budget is left.
;
; A float pair is worth less than a bit-vector pair of the same count: over
; bit-vectors the query's own (= a b) is a substitutable equality, so equality
; propagation collapses the actuals and the constraints dissolve before SAT;
; over floats it is FP_SMT_EQ, a predicate, and every constraint is paid in
; full. Floats used to be refused outright for that reason, which was right
; while the budget admitted a float declaration of up to 91 applications. At
; the current budget it admits at most 23, and across that band refusing costs
; more than it saves.
;
; Both halves of that are pinned here, and the first two blocks differ in
; exactly one token -- g's domain sort -- so neither can pass by accident.
;
; f is 190 pairs and g is 78, which do not both fit in 256. With g a float, f
; is taken and g is declined: a cheap float cannot take budget an expensive
; bit-vector declaration would have used. With g a bit-vector, plain
; cheapest-first applies and the answer inverts -- g is taken and f declined.
;
; The third block is the "out of whatever is left" half, and it is the one the
; ordering originally broke: f is 300 pairs and does not fit at all, and
; because floats queue behind every bit-vector signature, abandoning the scan
; at the first declaration that does not fit meant g was never reached. It was
; then reported as having had no comparable pairs on a line stating that it had
; ten.
;
; RUN: %solver -s --uninterpreted-functions --uf-ackermann=auto --incremental=off %s 2>&1 | %OutputCheck %s
; RUN: %solver -s --uninterpreted-functions --uf-ackermann=auto --incremental=on %s 2>&1 | %OutputCheck %s
;
; The report is ordered by declaration name, so reading one line after another
; is meaningful here rather than a reading of the allocator.
;
; CHECK: UF: eager selected f \(20 applications, 190 pairs estimated, 190 enumerated, 0 impossible, 190 constraints\)
; CHECK-NEXT: UF: eager declined-budget g \(13 applications, 78 pairs estimated\)
; CHECK-NEXT: UF: eager total 1/2 declarations, 190 constraints, budget 190/256 spent
; CHECK: ^sat
; CHECK: FLOAT-DONE
; CHECK: UF: eager declined-budget f \(20 applications, 190 pairs estimated\)
; CHECK-NEXT: UF: eager selected g \(13 applications, 78 pairs estimated, 78 enumerated, 0 impossible, 78 constraints\)
; CHECK-NEXT: UF: eager total 1/2 declarations, 78 constraints, budget 78/256 spent
; CHECK: ^sat
; CHECK: BITVECTOR-DONE
; CHECK: UF: eager declined-budget f \(25 applications, 300 pairs estimated\)
; CHECK-NEXT: UF: eager selected g \(5 applications, 10 pairs estimated, 10 enumerated, 0 impossible, 10 constraints\)
; CHECK-NEXT: UF: eager total 1/2 declarations, 10 constraints, budget 10/256 spent
; CHECK: ^sat
;
